@page "/docs/llms-txt"
@layout Layout.DocsLayout

<PageTitle>llms.txt · blux-ui</PageTitle>
<div class="pg-prose">
    <p class="pg-eyebrow">AI</p>
    <h1>llms.txt</h1>
    <p class="pg-lead">The documentation of this site as plain text, for language models that read a page instead of calling a tool.</p>

    <h2>Two files</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a>: an overview that follows the <a href="https://llmstxt.org">llms.txt convention</a>. A title, a one-paragraph summary, then a link and a one-line description for every guide and every component. A model reads it to decide which page it needs.</li>
        <li><a href="/llms-full.txt">/llms-full.txt</a>: the guides, then every component with its description, example source and API, in one file. Large, meant for tools that load everything into a context at once.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where they come from</h2>
    <p>
        Both are generated from the same bundle as the <a href="/docs/mcp">MCP server</a>, which is built from the docs catalog and the library itself, so the parameters listed
        there exist. Nothing is written by hand and nothing needs updating when a component changes.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting your own copy</h2>
    <p>
        The links in both files and in the answers of the MCP server are made from the public address of the site. Set it with the
        <code>Blux:SiteUrl</code> configuration key (the environment variable <code>Blux__SiteUrl</code>), for example <code>https://ui.example.com</code>:
        the files are then generated once at startup and never depend on the <code>Host</code> header of a request. Without it the address of the request is
        used, which is right for local use but lets any client choose the host written in the links.
    </p>
    <p>
        Behind a reverse proxy that terminates TLS, the app only sees http. The server reads <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>, <code>X-Forwarded-Host</code>
        and <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> from loopback proxies; list other proxy addresses in <code>Blux:KnownProxies</code> so links are https and the
        rate limit counts real clients. The <code>/mcp</code> endpoint allows 120 requests per minute per client IP
        (<code>Blux:Ai:RequestsPerMinute</code>) and stops a request after 30 seconds (<code>Blux:Ai:RequestTimeoutSeconds</code>).
    </p>

    <h2>Use them</h2>
    <p>Most agents accept a URL in the prompt or as a documentation source:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="Read https://your-docs-host/llms.txt, then build a settings page with Blux UI." />
    <p>
        Prefer the MCP server when you can: it returns only the component you ask for, and the exact parameters, instead of a whole file.
    </p>
</div>
